cartridge "PHOBIA/CLEVE BLAKEMORE/1988"

' PHOBIA
' A ColecoVision game based on "Phobia" by Cleve Blakemore, Ahoy! June 1988
' (Commodore 64 BASIC, disk ahoy1988-06). Made for Amy Studio by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/phobia.bas), the game is built for the ColecoVision: a
' tiled corridor in the Reality Institute, smooth 16 x 16 sprites (the
' runner, the laser bolt, the advancing wall), a sliding exit door, sound
' effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: a test of reason and reflexes. Two doors are named above the
' corridor, each a fate (WATER or AIR, DEMONS or TIGERS...). Choose the one
' you could survive with the stick (up: the top door, down: the bottom
' door) and run to the exit on the right. Jump (button) over the pits and
' the laser bolts, and keep ahead of the wall closing in from the left.
' The right fate opens the door; the wrong one, a pit, a bolt, or the wall
' pinning you at a door you never chose ends the game with the reason.
' Nine rooms make a graduate of the Institute.
'
' From the original (orig/phobia.bas): the 13 pairs of fates and the
' reasons (lines 1720-1840) and the congratulation and life-force messages;
' up and down choose a door (and may be changed until the door is reached),
' a choice is needed at the door (the runner bounces back without one); the
' run speed and the jump (8 steps up, 8 down, also while running); the
' laser bolt that keeps sweeping from right to left at the runner's
' height on rooms 2, 6, 7, 8 and 9; the pits (left one on rooms 4, 5, 7, 8,
' 9; right one on 3, 6, 7, 8, 9); the wall that comes from the left at 1
' (rooms 1-5), 2 (6-8) or 3 (9) pixels a step and pushes the runner
' ahead; one life (any death ends the game); the score of 50 x the pair's
' number + 250 x the room number for each room; nine rooms; the fanfare.
'
' Liberties: one BASIC pass of the C64 (about 20 a second) is spread over
' three frames, so everything moves smoothly at the original speeds; the
' jump is a smooth arc of 40 frames, 22 pixels high (the C64's 16 passes,
' 16 pixels, linear). The C64 picked a random pair each room (repeats
' possible); here the nine rooms of a game use nine different pairs. The
' laser waits a short random time off screen between sweeps (the C64's
' bolt wrapped round its 256-pixel register). A wall that pins a runner
' who has not chosen at the door crushes him (on the C64 the two sprites
' fought on). Collisions are boxes (the C64 used its sprite collision
' register; its pit sensors were invisible sprites). Added: two lines of
' controls under the corridor each room, a hint at the door when no fate
' is chosen, a WELL DONE line, the door sliding open as tiles, the rubble
' behind the wall. The reason is shown as a three-line message (the C64
' scrolled it across the top). A high score. GAME END then the title (the
' C64 restarted with RUN).
'
' Controls: either controller: the stick left and right runs, up and down
' choose a door, a side button jumps (hold it to keep jumping). On the
' title, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

